00:01:35.391 --> 00:01:36.680
everything if you want to go into

00:01:36.681 --> 00:01:39.230
more detail about
anything specific.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:41.285
Now, I'm just going to talk about

00:01:41.286 --> 00:01:44.275
basically what our group does.

00:01:44.276 --> 00:01:47.030
We're jointly located in

00:01:47.031 --> 00:01:49.940
Computer Science and
the Medical School at Yale,

00:01:49.941 --> 00:01:53.350
and we're basically
a machine learning

00:01:53.351 --> 00:01:55.320
or applied maths type group,

00:01:55.321 --> 00:01:58.310
and we develop techniques
that are meant to

00:01:58.311 --> 00:02:02.365
analyze complex biomedical data,

00:02:02.366 --> 00:02:04.430
and can usually or
often be applied to

00:02:04.431 --> 00:02:06.710
other types of data
because we believe

00:02:06.711 --> 00:02:08.330
that the bar for analyzing

00:02:08.331 --> 00:02:09.410
biomedical data is a little bit

00:02:09.411 --> 00:02:12.560
higher because there can
be all kinds of sparsity,

00:02:12.561 --> 00:02:15.655
missing information,
very, very noisy,

00:02:15.656 --> 00:02:17.680
a lot of nonlinear effects
and things like that,

00:02:17.681 --> 00:02:19.515
so we believe that if

00:02:19.516 --> 00:02:22.760
basically we do unsupervised
machine learning techniques,

00:02:22.761 --> 00:02:25.310
so if we're able to detect
those sorts of patterns and

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:29.060
hopefully we can detect
other sorts of patterns as well.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.474
So, as I was mentioning,

00:02:33.475 --> 00:02:36.530
our group develops
techniques that deal

00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:39.680
with data that can be
very high dimensional,

00:02:39.681 --> 00:02:42.059
so you're measuring a lot
of different features.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.000
Often, we apply our techniques
to single cell and

00:02:45.001 --> 00:02:49.080
sequencing data where the
features are transcript counts,

00:02:49.081 --> 00:02:51.390
mRNA transcript counts, but

00:02:51.391 --> 00:02:54.005
we've applied it on a
variety of other datasets.

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:55.680
Also high throughput, so

00:02:55.681 --> 00:02:57.320
there's a lot of different units.

00:02:57.321 --> 00:02:58.850
So, we've tended to work

00:02:58.851 --> 00:03:00.410
with single cell data
where the units are

00:03:00.411 --> 00:03:02.345
cells or data where

00:03:02.346 --> 00:03:05.620
the units are patients,
but there's a lot of them.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:10.455
There's a lot of heterogeneity
within the dataset,

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:13.040
which means that we
want to learn what

00:03:13.041 --> 00:03:17.270
the different ways that
the data itself is stratified,

00:03:17.271 --> 00:03:19.060
what the different patterns are,

00:03:19.061 --> 00:03:20.900
how much information and

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:23.645
redundancy there is
in the features.

00:03:23.646 --> 00:03:28.120
The challenges, this kind
of data can pose is,

00:03:28.121 --> 00:03:29.840
as I mentioned, there
could be sparsity.

00:03:29.841 --> 00:03:31.070
Even though there's
many, many features,

00:03:31.071 --> 00:03:32.435
a lot of them could be missing

00:03:32.436 --> 00:03:34.710
for a lot of the data points.

00:03:34.711 --> 00:03:35.960
There's a lot of noise,

00:03:35.961 --> 00:03:38.990
so there could be a very
extrinsic noise background,

00:03:38.991 --> 00:03:42.610
ambient noise or there could
be specific types of noise.

00:03:42.611 --> 00:03:45.530
There is nonlinearity in

00:03:45.531 --> 00:03:47.390
the shape of the data and

00:03:47.391 --> 00:03:49.250
how the features
relate to each other,

00:03:49.251 --> 00:03:52.000
and just the scale of it can
pose computational problems,

00:03:52.001 --> 00:03:55.540
so the algorithms
need to be scalable.

00:03:55.541 --> 00:03:57.510
So, with this kind of data,

00:03:57.511 --> 00:04:00.090
one question that
we often ask is,

00:04:00.091 --> 00:04:02.015
how do we look at this data?

00:04:02.016 --> 00:04:07.100
So, how you might have
a lot of data and often you

00:04:07.101 --> 00:04:09.170
might not even have
any ideas what

00:04:09.171 --> 00:04:12.170
your data can tell you or
what can even predict.

00:04:12.171 --> 00:04:13.695
So, in this circumstance,

00:04:13.696 --> 00:04:15.020
we have unsupervised techniques

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:16.400
that will help you just look

00:04:16.401 --> 00:04:17.720
at and explore the data and even

00:04:17.721 --> 00:04:20.180
generate hypotheses from it.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:25.100
So, some of the technologies
in biology that are

00:04:25.101 --> 00:04:27.620
generating this scale of data

00:04:27.621 --> 00:04:30.290
is mass cytometry or site
off as he mentioned,

00:04:30.291 --> 00:04:32.905
which generates a lot
of protein channels.

00:04:32.906 --> 00:04:36.555
RNA sequencing,
hich CTCR sequencing,

00:04:36.556 --> 00:04:38.525
which I know a lot
of you work with,

00:04:38.526 --> 00:04:40.670
and the list goes on.

00:04:40.671 --> 00:04:43.880
This is, we think this thing
just keeps exploding

00:04:43.881 --> 00:04:45.740
and there's more and
more technologies

00:04:45.741 --> 00:04:47.570
that are capable of
generating this.

00:04:47.571 --> 00:04:51.650
So, how do we deal
with these challenges?

00:04:51.651 --> 00:04:57.905
What are the themes that
we've seized upon in our lab

00:04:57.906 --> 00:05:00.050
is the fact that even though

00:05:00.051 --> 00:05:04.450
the data is measured in
a high dimensional space,

00:05:04.451 --> 00:05:07.280
it has intrinsically low
dimensional structure.

00:05:07.281 --> 00:05:10.765
So, for example, in
the gene sequence,

00:05:10.766 --> 00:05:11.875
RNA sequencing world,

00:05:11.876 --> 00:05:14.220
there are a lot of
genes that you measure.

00:05:14.221 --> 00:05:16.100
These genes have a high degree of

00:05:16.101 --> 00:05:18.620
informational redundancy
between them,

00:05:18.621 --> 00:05:20.780
and as a result the cell types

00:05:20.781 --> 00:05:22.320
and states are constraints.

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:24.450
So, there's an intrinsically

00:05:24.451 --> 00:05:26.300
lower dimensional shape here,

00:05:26.301 --> 00:05:28.370
so the ambient space

00:05:28.371 --> 00:05:30.985
was a lot bigger than
the intrinsic shape,

00:05:30.986 --> 00:05:35.645
and so, that's one of the
principles we use in our lab.

00:05:35.646 --> 00:05:38.955
How do we learn this lower
dimensional intrinsic shape?

00:05:38.956 --> 00:05:41.300
As you can see, this
shape can be non-linear.

00:05:41.301 --> 00:05:47.750
How do we understand
the shape and ED?

00:05:47.751 --> 00:05:51.170
One of the fields in math that

00:05:51.171 --> 00:05:55.055
we use a lot is
graph signal processing,

00:05:55.056 --> 00:05:58.790
where there's in this theory
of manifolds and we show

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.230
that we can also implement

00:06:00.231 --> 00:06:02.265
the same concept
with Deep Learning,

00:06:02.266 --> 00:06:05.550
where not only does
the data have shape,

00:06:05.551 --> 00:06:09.295
but there is some
smoothness in it.

00:06:09.296 --> 00:06:12.725
So, the smoothness or

00:06:12.726 --> 00:06:15.170
the locality and
the data can really help

00:06:15.171 --> 00:06:17.840
you learn and
understand this shape.

00:06:17.841 --> 00:06:20.530
I'll give you an example here,

00:06:20.531 --> 00:06:23.420
smoothness meaning your
data points are occupying

00:06:23.421 --> 00:06:26.810
the state space in
some semi regular fashion,

00:06:26.811 --> 00:06:29.000
even if they're not
uniformly sampled across it,

00:06:29.001 --> 00:06:30.920
you have samples in
different parts of

00:06:30.921 --> 00:06:33.010
it so that you can learn
something about it.

00:06:33.011 --> 00:06:34.900
It doesn't have to
be one smooth shape,

00:06:34.901 --> 00:06:36.525
it can be a collection of them.

00:06:36.526 --> 00:06:39.080
So basically, if you have

00:06:39.081 --> 00:06:42.150
this highly non-linear lower
dimensional structure,

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:43.745
how do you learn it?

00:06:43.746 --> 00:06:47.210
The very basic way
you learn it is you

00:06:47.211 --> 00:06:50.945
look for very similar data
points to every data point.

00:06:50.946 --> 00:06:52.815
To some level of granularity,

00:06:52.816 --> 00:06:54.920
even if you have high
dimensions and noise,

00:06:54.921 --> 00:06:56.360
you should be able
to tell data points

00:06:56.361 --> 00:06:57.500
that are very similar to

00:06:57.501 --> 00:07:01.165
your point in a very local way.

00:07:01.166 --> 00:07:03.020
So, if you're trying to look

00:07:03.021 --> 00:07:04.700
for points that
are very far away,

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:05.750
you can get lost because,

00:07:05.751 --> 00:07:09.550
unless you know this is
structured like a Swiss roll,

00:07:09.551 --> 00:07:12.070
you might not
understand the points,

00:07:12.071 --> 00:07:14.270
and the different arms
are actually far away,

00:07:14.271 --> 00:07:15.740
but you can understand
which points

00:07:15.741 --> 00:07:17.370
are very close to each other.

00:07:17.371 --> 00:07:18.675
So, once you have

00:07:18.676 --> 00:07:20.090
points that are very
close to each other,

00:07:20.091 --> 00:07:21.860
you can basically
take a walk by taking

00:07:21.861 --> 00:07:24.020
small steps to local neighbors,

00:07:24.021 --> 00:07:25.625
and this walk will
somehow help you

00:07:25.626 --> 00:07:28.070
clarify the shape of
the data and find

00:07:28.071 --> 00:07:31.520
this highly nonlinear
intrinsic dimension

00:07:31.521 --> 00:07:33.520
that the data follows.

00:07:33.521 --> 00:07:37.310
So, when you walk in
small steps you can

00:07:37.311 --> 00:07:40.650
define Markov Matrix that says,

00:07:40.651 --> 00:07:42.320
starting from any point,

00:07:42.321 --> 00:07:44.120
you're going to transition
to other points

00:07:44.121 --> 00:07:46.140
based on your measure
of similarity.

00:07:46.141 --> 00:07:47.270
That measure of
similarity doesn't

00:07:47.271 --> 00:07:49.430
have to be necessarily
very perfect.

00:07:49.431 --> 00:07:51.000
It can be pretty rough,

00:07:51.001 --> 00:07:52.700
but as you walk longer distances,

00:07:52.701 --> 00:07:53.950
the probabilities add up,

00:07:53.951 --> 00:07:55.370
so that you hone,

00:07:55.371 --> 00:07:56.450
the longer you walk,

00:07:56.451 --> 00:07:59.610
the more you hone in on the main
dimensions of the shape.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:03.620
So, this concept is

00:08:03.621 --> 00:08:06.920
called data diffusion
or a random walk

00:08:06.921 --> 00:08:09.350
and this data diffusion type

00:08:09.351 --> 00:08:10.370
concept where you find

00:08:10.371 --> 00:08:12.510
these main non-linear
Eigen dimensions,

00:08:12.511 --> 00:08:16.600
turns out to be the basis of
graphs signal processing.

00:08:16.601 --> 00:08:20.554
So, we used this concept for

00:08:20.555 --> 00:08:23.300
the first project that David

00:08:23.301 --> 00:08:26.700
and I did together that was
just published in Cell,

00:08:26.701 --> 00:08:28.700
and it's an algorithm
called MAGIC,

00:08:28.701 --> 00:08:31.010
and what MAGIC shows

00:08:31.011 --> 00:08:33.470
is that by learning
this manifold of the data,

00:08:33.471 --> 00:08:38.660
you can restore
values to cells that

00:08:38.661 --> 00:08:40.850
were missing or you can

00:08:40.851 --> 00:08:43.990
denoise cells such that you
can further process them,

00:08:43.991 --> 00:08:46.070
so restoring data basically

00:08:46.071 --> 00:08:48.325
to the mean manifold dimensions.

00:08:48.326 --> 00:08:52.910
The motivating problem was in
single cell RNA sequencing,

00:08:52.911 --> 00:08:54.680
which came out about
three years ago,

00:08:54.681 --> 00:08:56.810
you have a salvaging
count matrix,

00:08:56.811 --> 00:08:58.180
but most of it is missing,

00:08:58.181 --> 00:09:01.714
just by the way
RNA capture works,

00:09:01.715 --> 00:09:03.950
it's like reaching into your cell

00:09:03.951 --> 00:09:06.125
and grabbing five to
10 percent of the transcripts,

00:09:06.126 --> 00:09:08.270
and the rest of them
you leave in the cell.

00:09:08.271 --> 00:09:12.340
So, given this, a lot of what
I've done with site off,

00:09:12.341 --> 00:09:13.640
for example, in my post-doc,

00:09:13.641 --> 00:09:14.960
which is try to learn

00:09:14.961 --> 00:09:16.430
relationships and signaling

00:09:16.431 --> 00:09:18.140
the relationships
between proteins,

00:09:18.141 --> 00:09:19.620
could no longer be done.

00:09:19.621 --> 00:09:21.625
So, if you're looking
per gene now,

00:09:21.626 --> 00:09:23.240
and you're trying to
get information from

00:09:23.241 --> 00:09:25.550
your 20,000 genes as far as

00:09:25.551 --> 00:09:28.310
with gene has a relationship

00:09:28.311 --> 00:09:30.230
or correlation with another gene,

00:09:30.231 --> 00:09:32.270
it becomes very hard
to discern this

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:34.415
because you're not seeing

00:09:34.416 --> 00:09:36.330
both the genes in
the same cell at all.

00:09:36.331 --> 00:09:38.870
So, if you only see
one gene in 10 percent of

00:09:38.871 --> 00:09:40.050
the cells and another gene

00:09:40.051 --> 00:09:41.675
in another 10 percent in cells,

00:09:41.676 --> 00:09:43.370
the likelihood of you
seeing both genes in

00:09:43.371 --> 00:09:45.050
the same cell is one in

00:09:45.051 --> 00:09:48.380
one hundred and so a lot of
this data is dropped out,

00:09:48.381 --> 00:09:50.930
and you can't do that type
of gene regulatory analysis

00:09:50.931 --> 00:09:54.630
that seems to be promised
by the technology.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:58.075
So, the steps of

00:09:58.076 --> 00:09:59.680
MAGIC basically are that you

00:09:59.681 --> 00:10:01.390
start with a cell by gene matrix,

00:10:01.391 --> 00:10:03.650
you calculate
distances, but again,

00:10:03.651 --> 00:10:05.290
you don't trust
faraway distances.

00:10:05.291 --> 00:10:07.090
So, you localize the distances by

00:10:07.091 --> 00:10:09.775
passing it through
a Gaussian affinity.

00:10:09.776 --> 00:10:12.600
So, that gives you
very local neighbors and

00:10:12.601 --> 00:10:14.080
then you Markov normalize it

00:10:14.081 --> 00:10:15.880
to create a Random-Walk matrix.

00:10:15.881 --> 00:10:18.230
Then you exponentiate
this matrix to compute

00:10:18.231 --> 00:10:21.520
the probabilities of
longer Random-Walks.

00:10:21.521 --> 00:10:24.090
Then, this has honed

00:10:24.091 --> 00:10:26.910
in on the main
dimensions of the data.

00:10:26.911 --> 00:10:28.470
So, if you, eigen decompose

00:10:28.471 --> 00:10:30.230
it then you'd get what's
called a diffusion math,

00:10:30.231 --> 00:10:31.950
but we don't actually
eigen decompose it.

00:10:31.951 --> 00:10:33.650
You basically project
this operator

00:10:33.651 --> 00:10:35.145
onto the data to learn

00:10:35.146 --> 00:10:36.670
a weighted average of

00:10:36.671 --> 00:10:40.430
manifold intrinsic neighbors
to get the imputed data.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:44.050
So, you basically
have data before

00:10:44.051 --> 00:10:46.910
MAGIC and after several
steps of imputation,

00:10:46.911 --> 00:10:49.875
when you project, you get
your restored data after MAGIC.

00:10:49.876 --> 00:10:52.180
You can see some of
the other types of

00:10:52.181 --> 00:10:53.190
noise in the data besides

00:10:53.191 --> 00:10:55.120
missing values are also restored.

00:10:55.121 --> 00:10:58.895
What the subsequent steps
of diffusion do are,

00:10:58.896 --> 00:11:02.200
it connects the manifold
more and more globally.

00:11:02.201 --> 00:11:05.510
So, you get more global
directions, and as you do this,

00:11:05.511 --> 00:11:09.370
you take off the low noise
dimensions and you hone in on

00:11:09.371 --> 00:11:11.550
the main manifold
dimensions and you see

00:11:11.551 --> 00:11:14.790
restored relationships per gene.

00:11:14.791 --> 00:11:17.160
It can restore it
to nonlinear values

00:11:17.161 --> 00:11:18.690
because you're not imposing

00:11:18.691 --> 00:11:20.660
any global constraint on

00:11:20.661 --> 00:11:23.320
the shape of the relationship
or what you impute.

00:11:23.321 --> 00:11:24.250
&gt;&gt; I have a question.

00:11:24.251 --> 00:11:25.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:11:25.121 --> 00:11:28.060
&gt;&gt; What is this?
What point single column?

00:11:28.061 --> 00:11:29.215
&gt;&gt; It's a single cell.

00:11:29.216 --> 00:11:30.925
&gt;&gt; It's a single cell,

00:11:30.926 --> 00:11:32.935
but what's the X-axis and Y?

00:11:32.936 --> 00:11:34.090
&gt;&gt; They're two genes.

00:11:34.091 --> 00:11:36.520
Two random genes you
can think of it as.

00:11:36.521 --> 00:11:38.715
&gt;&gt; So, this is single
start a sequencing,

00:11:38.716 --> 00:11:43.275
so you count molecules
for genes in a cell.

00:11:43.276 --> 00:11:47.120
So, these are molecule counts,
observed molecules counts.

00:11:47.121 --> 00:11:50.615
&gt;&gt; I tried to understand
what's on the X-axis,

