00:13:39.536 --> 00:13:41.090
So, then you'r interested.

00:13:41.091 --> 00:13:42.220
&gt;&gt; Except for the
interesting, sorry

00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:43.600
the interesting genes are lowly

00:13:43.601 --> 00:13:45.755
expressed like
transcription factors.

00:13:45.756 --> 00:13:46.840
&gt;&gt; Surface markers.

00:13:46.841 --> 00:13:48.615
&gt;&gt; Interesting stuff that
is highly expressed.

00:13:48.616 --> 00:13:48.760
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:48.761 --> 00:13:49.560
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately, yeah.

00:13:49.561 --> 00:13:51.210
&gt;&gt; Actin is highly expressive.

00:13:51.211 --> 00:13:52.575
&gt;&gt; Actin yeah, as well as actin.

00:13:52.576 --> 00:13:55.710
&gt;&gt; So, yeah, I know
exactly cell surface markers

00:13:55.711 --> 00:13:57.850
because then you can use them
to sort and study cells,

00:13:57.851 --> 00:13:59.065
those are exactly missing.

00:13:59.066 --> 00:14:02.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Before your imputation.

00:14:02.661 --> 00:14:05.310
Did that help answer
your question?

00:14:05.311 --> 00:14:05.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:14:05.491 --> 00:14:07.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I'm not assuming

00:14:07.151 --> 00:14:08.610
you know what these
relationships look like,

00:14:08.611 --> 00:14:10.045
although if you know about

00:14:10.046 --> 00:14:11.860
epithelial-mesenchymal
transition.

00:14:11.861 --> 00:14:14.750
Breast cancer you know that
this is correct direction of

00:14:14.751 --> 00:14:16.290
these relationships
because Snail is

00:14:16.291 --> 00:14:17.340
a transcription factor and

00:14:17.341 --> 00:14:19.425
Vimentin is a mesenchymal marker.

00:14:19.426 --> 00:14:20.860
So, when Snail goes on,

00:14:20.861 --> 00:14:23.680
Vimentin goes up and then
E-Cadherin and Vimentin

00:14:23.681 --> 00:14:26.560
are markers of epithelial
versus mesenchymal state,

00:14:26.561 --> 00:14:28.085
so they're not on
it the same time.

00:14:28.086 --> 00:14:29.650
&gt;&gt; By the way, I don't know
if you have a slide of

00:14:29.651 --> 00:14:32.710
that. We did try.

00:14:32.711 --> 00:14:34.780
&gt;&gt; I have a slide of that.

00:14:34.781 --> 00:14:36.670
&gt;&gt; We did try matrix
completion techniques.

00:14:36.671 --> 00:14:37.240
&gt;&gt; It doesn't work.

00:14:37.241 --> 00:14:40.310
&gt;&gt; Then we failed on
the same date just so you know.

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:43.750
&gt;&gt; So, we always try to

00:14:43.751 --> 00:14:46.620
find ways to validate it
computationally and otherwise.

00:14:46.621 --> 00:14:49.350
So, here we had a gene of worms

00:14:49.351 --> 00:14:52.390
that were developing
for the first,

00:14:52.391 --> 00:14:56.865
I think it's 48 hours and
they had bulk sequencing.

00:14:56.866 --> 00:14:59.900
But there was a natural
manifold structure

00:14:59.901 --> 00:15:02.460
that we knew about.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:06.300
We artificially dropped this data

00:15:06.301 --> 00:15:07.730
out and we re-imputed it

00:15:07.731 --> 00:15:11.705
with MAGIC and denoised
it as you can see.

00:15:11.706 --> 00:15:16.750
The original relationships
got totally messed up,

00:15:16.751 --> 00:15:19.525
most of it sunk down to zero.

00:15:19.526 --> 00:15:22.500
Then you re-impute it with
MAGIC and you see that

00:15:22.501 --> 00:15:24.615
the original relationship is

00:15:24.616 --> 00:15:27.710
restored or even
further clarified.

00:15:27.711 --> 00:15:31.075
So, why is this
interesting to us?

00:15:31.076 --> 00:15:34.220
Because you can learn a lot
about individual genes now,

00:15:34.221 --> 00:15:36.150
you can look at
their pairwise relationships.

00:15:36.151 --> 00:15:40.700
Their individual probability
distributions are

00:15:40.701 --> 00:15:44.550
re-represented and
you can distinguish

00:15:44.551 --> 00:15:46.470
between different
populations and how much

00:15:46.471 --> 00:15:48.970
of a particular gene
is represented.

00:15:48.971 --> 00:15:51.880
In addition to looking
at individual genes,

00:15:51.881 --> 00:15:54.970
the overall data is restored.

00:15:54.971 --> 00:15:57.500
So, this is before MAGIC
and afterwards you see

00:15:57.501 --> 00:16:01.125
a very clear structure
of this data.

00:16:01.126 --> 00:16:04.990
This is again, a data
that's mimicking

00:16:04.991 --> 00:16:06.670
the epithelial-mesenchymal
transition

00:16:06.671 --> 00:16:09.205
on breast cancer cells.

00:16:09.206 --> 00:16:12.475
So, we see different structures
corresponding to

00:16:12.476 --> 00:16:14.460
the yellow is
mesenchymal cells that

00:16:14.461 --> 00:16:17.440
successfully transition
for example.

00:16:17.441 --> 00:16:20.660
So, one thing we can do after
MAGIC that we completely

00:16:20.661 --> 00:16:24.280
couldn't is do something called
the archetypal analysis.

00:16:24.281 --> 00:16:26.540
So, there's data before
MAGIC and after.

00:16:26.541 --> 00:16:29.550
The main idea is to fit a
polytope to the extreme ends of

00:16:29.551 --> 00:16:33.165
the data to figure out what's
creating the overall shape.

00:16:33.166 --> 00:16:36.660
When we did that, we
were able to identify

00:16:36.661 --> 00:16:38.960
extreme end points of their shape

00:16:38.961 --> 00:16:41.555
that correspond to
chromatin modification.

00:16:41.556 --> 00:16:44.490
So, for example now,

00:16:44.491 --> 00:16:46.630
biologist could look at
these intermediate states

00:16:46.631 --> 00:16:48.655
that people hadn't really
considered that much

00:16:48.656 --> 00:16:50.030
and see that there is

00:16:50.031 --> 00:16:52.080
a one programmable chromatin

00:16:52.081 --> 00:16:53.630
modification that's going on.

00:16:53.631 --> 00:16:55.955
So, a lot of times
people hone in on

00:16:55.956 --> 00:16:57.610
the first part of this which are

00:16:57.611 --> 00:16:59.100
epithelial cells or the last part

00:16:59.101 --> 00:17:00.290
which are mesenchymal cells.

00:17:00.291 --> 00:17:02.050
They're not really
sure how these cells

00:17:02.051 --> 00:17:03.180
transition through

00:17:03.181 --> 00:17:05.160
the intermediate
populations and that's

00:17:05.161 --> 00:17:08.210
something that you can
start to get a picture

00:17:08.211 --> 00:17:12.265
of after you've restored
all your gene values.

00:17:12.266 --> 00:17:15.045
This is comparing MAGIC against

00:17:15.046 --> 00:17:19.185
matrix completion and
low-rank approximation methods.

00:17:19.186 --> 00:17:23.120
So, you can see that the NNMC

00:17:23.121 --> 00:17:27.335
is nuclear norm base
matrix completion,

00:17:27.336 --> 00:17:29.140
Candace-based method and it

00:17:29.141 --> 00:17:31.470
isn't performs super well here.

00:17:31.471 --> 00:17:34.700
Low-rank approximation
actually does somewhat better,

00:17:34.701 --> 00:17:35.740
but it's a linear methods,

00:17:35.741 --> 00:17:36.880
so it's not completely

00:17:36.881 --> 00:17:39.240
denoising in the
non-linear directions.

00:17:39.241 --> 00:17:42.440
Then you see MAGIC
resource the data shape

00:17:42.441 --> 00:17:46.035
to something that's
identifiable to us.

00:17:46.036 --> 00:17:48.780
We measured it against
a lot of other ways,

00:17:48.781 --> 00:17:50.090
so if people often
ask us do you need

00:17:50.091 --> 00:17:52.965
this sort of complicated
Random-Walk technique,

00:17:52.966 --> 00:17:54.200
can you just do this by

00:17:54.201 --> 00:17:55.910
imputing with KNN
and you see it's not

00:17:55.911 --> 00:17:57.320
nearly as good and if you impute

00:17:57.321 --> 00:17:59.100
with just your
k-nearest neighbors.

00:17:59.101 --> 00:18:00.740
The global manifold connections

00:18:00.741 --> 00:18:02.600
actually do make a difference.

00:18:02.601 --> 00:18:04.615
When you're learning the shapes,

00:18:04.616 --> 00:18:06.230
and you can't use

00:18:06.231 --> 00:18:09.220
individual diffusion
components or

00:18:09.221 --> 00:18:11.580
anything like that
to get the same.

00:18:11.581 --> 00:18:15.190
So, it can't be just simple
smoothing in your graph,

00:18:15.191 --> 00:18:18.585
so the manifold learning
actually makes a difference.

00:18:18.586 --> 00:18:21.030
So, after we did this project,

00:18:21.031 --> 00:18:23.910
so basically what we did was,

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:28.180
we restored the gene values.

00:18:28.181 --> 00:18:30.810
We thought we could do
similar inference because

00:18:30.811 --> 00:18:33.370
we've learned the gene cell
graph on external variables,

00:18:33.371 --> 00:18:35.300
and we thought this
could really help us.

00:18:35.301 --> 00:18:38.050
Because the single-cell
RNA sequencing data

00:18:38.051 --> 00:18:40.190
now is not only in
a single condition,

00:18:40.191 --> 00:18:42.010
but it can be generated in

00:18:42.011 --> 00:18:43.120
multiple conditions and now

00:18:43.121 --> 00:18:44.600
people want you to
compare conditions.

00:18:44.601 --> 00:18:45.980
So, I have the single-cell data

00:18:45.981 --> 00:18:47.475
and I have it with perturbation.

00:18:47.476 --> 00:18:49.835
So, what's the real difference?

00:18:49.836 --> 00:18:53.125
Measuring the difference
actually pretty hard

00:18:53.126 --> 00:18:55.570
because there's all this noise

00:18:55.571 --> 00:18:58.030
that occludes the difference.

00:18:58.031 --> 00:19:00.320
But also, the measurement effect

00:19:00.321 --> 00:19:03.885
itself could be weak or
could be measured weakly.

00:19:03.886 --> 00:19:07.605
So, we thought we'd
actually treat

00:19:07.606 --> 00:19:11.635
this experimental
variable itself as

00:19:11.636 --> 00:19:13.330
a similar signal as we treated

00:19:13.331 --> 00:19:15.680
a gene and use the same sort of

00:19:15.681 --> 00:19:18.125
graph manifold
denoising technique

00:19:18.126 --> 00:19:20.240
on an external variable.

00:19:20.241 --> 00:19:22.440
But it's configured
a little bit differently

00:19:22.441 --> 00:19:24.185
because we don't know

00:19:24.186 --> 00:19:26.185
some things about
the experimental variable.

00:19:26.186 --> 00:19:29.090
So, this is a technique
that's in preparation,

00:19:29.091 --> 00:19:31.415
so David is supervising

00:19:31.416 --> 00:19:34.445
this project and Jay and
Dan are working on it.

00:19:34.446 --> 00:19:36.785
The idea is to
smooth an external,

00:19:36.786 --> 00:19:39.755
perhaps experimental
label on the data graph

00:19:39.756 --> 00:19:43.115
to impute continuity
and infer associations.

00:19:43.116 --> 00:19:44.980
So, the idea here is you have

00:19:44.981 --> 00:19:47.065
cells from condition
one and condition two.

00:19:47.066 --> 00:19:48.510
But you've learned
your graph from

00:19:48.511 --> 00:19:52.320
single-cell RNA sequencing data

00:19:52.321 --> 00:19:55.515
from the whole data on the genes.

00:19:55.516 --> 00:19:58.350
Now, you can interpolate
the labels to

00:19:58.351 --> 00:20:01.505
get a continuous variable

00:20:01.506 --> 00:20:03.200
that can now give you

00:20:03.201 --> 00:20:05.270
some enhanced abilities
to tell the difference

00:20:05.271 --> 00:20:07.460
between the two conditions.

00:20:07.461 --> 00:20:09.890
So, one thing it can
do is it can improve

00:20:09.891 --> 00:20:11.210
your ability to correlate

00:20:11.211 --> 00:20:13.565
variables with
the experimental condition.

00:20:13.566 --> 00:20:16.315
So, before you had
these discrete labels,

00:20:16.316 --> 00:20:19.180
between condition one
and condition two,

00:20:19.181 --> 00:20:22.160
there really a significant
difference, can you tell?

00:20:22.161 --> 00:20:23.750
Often you can only tell if it's

00:20:23.751 --> 00:20:25.730
a monotonic relationship
like in A but

00:20:25.731 --> 00:20:27.994
you can't tell
a non-monotonic relationship

00:20:27.995 --> 00:20:30.035
like you have in B.

00:20:30.036 --> 00:20:33.210
So, after you, we
call these melding,

00:20:33.211 --> 00:20:34.300
we've turned it into a verb,

00:20:34.301 --> 00:20:35.700
so we're melding variables.

00:20:35.701 --> 00:20:37.190
After we MELD the variable,

00:20:37.191 --> 00:20:38.990
we infer this light
and dimension that

00:20:38.991 --> 00:20:41.120
seems much easier to track,

00:20:41.121 --> 00:20:43.980
to see if it correlates with
the experimental condition.

00:20:43.981 --> 00:20:45.100
So, you can think of

00:20:45.101 --> 00:20:48.200
the MELD dimension as a
continuous experimental variable.

00:20:48.201 --> 00:20:49.925
The other thing you can do

00:20:49.926 --> 00:20:51.670
is help us identify cell types.

00:20:51.671 --> 00:20:53.370
So, if you have condition
A and condition

00:20:53.371 --> 00:20:55.130
B and you mix samples from it,

00:20:55.131 --> 00:20:57.230
you can see which cell types are

00:20:57.231 --> 00:21:00.480
enriched in condition
A versus condition B.

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:03.860
So, some of the math behind it,

00:21:03.861 --> 00:21:06.030
they're also using
graph signal processing.

00:21:06.031 --> 00:21:07.580
So the idea in
graph signal processing

00:21:07.581 --> 00:21:08.750
is you have a graph,

00:21:08.751 --> 00:21:09.830
and you're treating
this variable as

00:21:09.831 --> 00:21:11.130
a signal on that graph.

00:21:11.131 --> 00:21:12.530
What you're actually doing is

00:21:12.531 --> 00:21:15.645
you're low-pass
filtering this graph.

00:21:15.646 --> 00:21:18.660
So, like before,

00:21:18.661 --> 00:21:20.945
when you power the matrix,

00:21:20.946 --> 00:21:23.010
what you're actually
doing is you're keeping

00:21:23.011 --> 00:21:24.940
the high eigen vectors
of this Markov matrix,

00:21:24.941 --> 00:21:26.340
which range from one to zero,

00:21:26.341 --> 00:21:28.255
but the properties
of Markov matrices.

00:21:28.256 --> 00:21:30.060
So, something is one to zero,

00:21:30.061 --> 00:21:31.730
and you raise it to a power,

00:21:31.731 --> 00:21:34.175
it goes down unless
it's close to one.

00:21:34.176 --> 00:21:37.130
That's the property that
you're using that smoothing

00:21:37.131 --> 00:21:40.120
on this graph or smoothing
on the Laplacian.

00:21:40.121 --> 00:21:41.350
So, you see that before the

00:21:41.351 --> 00:21:43.630
smoothing the signal is noisy,

00:21:43.631 --> 00:21:45.575
has all these high
frequency components.

00:21:45.576 --> 00:21:47.695
As you smooth more and more,

00:21:47.696 --> 00:21:49.930
you hone in on the
low-frequency components.

00:21:49.931 --> 00:21:51.550
So, you're smoothing
the external variable

00:21:51.551 --> 00:21:53.060
on the main directions
of the graph

00:21:53.061 --> 00:21:54.850
assuming that the main
directions of the graph

00:21:54.851 --> 00:21:57.400
sort of represent the difference.

00:21:57.401 --> 00:21:59.680
&gt;&gt; What is T?

00:21:59.681 --> 00:22:01.420
&gt;&gt; T would be how much
you power the matrix,

00:22:01.421 --> 00:22:03.670
even though in this setup
we actually don't power it.

00:22:03.671 --> 00:22:05.710
We have it as
a convex optimization

00:22:05.711 --> 00:22:07.985
that trades smoothing
with reconstruction.

