00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:07.870
&gt;&gt; So, another project that we

00:29:07.871 --> 00:29:10.990
did that uses the same ideas is,

00:29:10.991 --> 00:29:12.510
we realized that we have of

00:29:12.511 --> 00:29:14.480
this high-dimensional
structure but we don't

00:29:14.481 --> 00:29:16.090
have a very good way of

00:29:16.091 --> 00:29:18.850
visualizing this structure
for exploratory purposes.

00:29:18.851 --> 00:29:20.960
So, we, in some cases,

00:29:20.961 --> 00:29:22.910
when new genes we
were interested in,

00:29:22.911 --> 00:29:24.940
so we're visualizing
biaxial plots.

00:29:24.941 --> 00:29:27.800
In other case, we visualize PCA.

00:29:27.801 --> 00:29:29.720
But it came to
our attention that all of

00:29:29.721 --> 00:29:32.255
these methods leave out
a lot from the data.

00:29:32.256 --> 00:29:34.510
So, because we learned
the whole manifold of the data,

00:29:34.511 --> 00:29:36.370
there should be
some way of visualizing

00:29:36.371 --> 00:29:38.715
it that preserved
structure a bit more.

00:29:38.716 --> 00:29:41.325
This led to a project
called PHATE.

00:29:41.326 --> 00:29:43.785
That's under revision now.

00:29:43.786 --> 00:29:46.950
So, the idea of PHATE is that

00:29:46.951 --> 00:29:50.890
because we have
a natural understanding

00:29:50.891 --> 00:29:52.455
of the shape of the data,

00:29:52.456 --> 00:29:56.080
we can squeeze it into lower
dimensions in such a way

00:29:56.081 --> 00:30:00.410
that captures both local and
global structure,

00:30:00.411 --> 00:30:03.494
and therefore trajectory
structures in biology,

00:30:03.495 --> 00:30:06.620
but also disparates
cluster structures.

00:30:06.621 --> 00:30:09.510
We realize that not a lot of
other visualization methods

00:30:09.511 --> 00:30:13.345
really preserved
structure in this way.

00:30:13.346 --> 00:30:17.040
So, I'm going to show this
both on artificial data

00:30:17.041 --> 00:30:18.210
and data generated from

00:30:18.211 --> 00:30:20.450
an embryoid body
differentiation system,

00:30:20.451 --> 00:30:21.995
where you start out with human

00:30:21.996 --> 00:30:24.520
embryonic stem cells and then you

00:30:24.521 --> 00:30:26.820
differentiate them
into variety of

00:30:26.821 --> 00:30:29.485
different lineages over
a 30-day time course.

00:30:29.486 --> 00:30:31.720
So, this artificial tree data,

00:30:31.721 --> 00:30:33.600
which my lab sometimes
calls the dump tree,

00:30:33.601 --> 00:30:36.175
but you will find out
the dump tree is harder than

00:30:36.176 --> 00:30:39.220
it looks as you see here.

00:30:39.221 --> 00:30:42.780
So, if you take this dump tree
and you add noise to it,

00:30:42.781 --> 00:30:44.420
and you rotate in high
dimensions and you look to

00:30:44.421 --> 00:30:46.350
see if you can visualize it.

00:30:46.351 --> 00:30:48.500
If you try to
visualize it with PCA,

00:30:48.501 --> 00:30:50.250
you're missing a lot
of the structure

00:30:50.251 --> 00:30:51.760
in the dump tree even

00:30:51.761 --> 00:30:53.105
though it picked up

00:30:53.106 --> 00:30:55.860
the global directionality
in some way.

00:30:55.861 --> 00:30:57.530
It is going from blue to green,

00:30:57.531 --> 00:31:01.985
but it's losing a lot of
the branching structure.

00:31:01.986 --> 00:31:04.720
tSNE seems to be
all the range in this field.

00:31:04.721 --> 00:31:06.890
I'm not sure, maybe
in other fields too.

00:31:06.891 --> 00:31:11.325
But tSNE, actually,
shatters the data

00:31:11.326 --> 00:31:13.710
in a bad way because
it only cares

00:31:13.711 --> 00:31:17.120
about keeping stochastic
neighbors together.

00:31:17.121 --> 00:31:20.520
We believe that if you
don't keep just neighbors

00:31:20.521 --> 00:31:24.224
but keep manifold intrinsic
relationships altogether,

00:31:24.225 --> 00:31:25.030
that's a better way of

00:31:25.031 --> 00:31:26.700
preserving the structure
in the data.

00:31:26.701 --> 00:31:28.700
You see, PHATE can preserve

00:31:28.701 --> 00:31:32.100
the data but it can also
denoise it, which is important.

00:31:32.101 --> 00:31:36.150
So, you see the lines that
PHATE gives are thinner

00:31:36.151 --> 00:31:41.055
and also denoise it in the
manifold intrinsic dimensions.

00:31:41.056 --> 00:31:44.860
So, here's PHATE shown on.

00:31:44.861 --> 00:31:46.555
We especially hope that

00:31:46.556 --> 00:31:47.860
a lot of people start
using this because

00:31:47.861 --> 00:31:50.615
this is basically can work
on any any type of data.

00:31:50.616 --> 00:31:52.070
Here, you see the embryonic

00:31:52.071 --> 00:31:54.180
stem cell differentiation system.

00:31:54.181 --> 00:31:57.520
The red to the left is

00:31:57.521 --> 00:31:59.590
all embryonic stem cells

00:31:59.591 --> 00:32:01.450
because that's from
day zero to three,

00:32:01.451 --> 00:32:04.215
and we started with
embryonic stem cells.

00:32:04.216 --> 00:32:06.480
As the days go on,

00:32:06.481 --> 00:32:08.860
you create the lineages
that are colored in

00:32:08.861 --> 00:32:12.050
orange that we picked up
on day six through nine.

00:32:12.051 --> 00:32:13.980
Then, finally, the blue
or the latest that we

00:32:13.981 --> 00:32:17.160
generated 24 through 27.

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:22.795
Again, you see PCA picks up
at least this time label.

00:32:22.796 --> 00:32:24.920
The fact that
there's fewer cells,

00:32:24.921 --> 00:32:28.260
so there's less space
occupied by the red than

00:32:28.261 --> 00:32:29.270
the green and blue because

00:32:29.271 --> 00:32:31.670
it's branching out as lineages.

00:32:31.671 --> 00:32:35.455
But the internal structure
is not clarified.

00:32:35.456 --> 00:32:37.390
The tSNE doesn't even do that.

00:32:37.391 --> 00:32:40.390
So, this is very bad for
preserving continuum structure.

00:32:40.391 --> 00:32:43.779
So, you see the red is shattered

00:32:43.780 --> 00:32:45.840
out on either sides of the

00:32:45.841 --> 00:32:48.010
green and the global
relationships

00:32:48.011 --> 00:32:49.160
are all completely messed up.

00:32:49.161 --> 00:32:51.510
So, can I trust this

00:32:51.511 --> 00:32:53.500
for understanding really

00:32:53.501 --> 00:32:55.975
the overall global
structure of your data?

00:32:55.976 --> 00:32:59.005
Diffusion maps which is
somewhat based on this,

00:32:59.006 --> 00:33:01.260
is based on this concept
but it's an older method.

00:33:01.261 --> 00:33:04.410
It doesn't preserve in a way
that you can visualize.

00:33:04.411 --> 00:33:06.900
Basically, what it's doing
is it's taking each branch,

00:33:06.901 --> 00:33:08.670
each trajectory, each
cluster and putting it out

00:33:08.671 --> 00:33:10.545
to a different dimension,
so you need to visualize,

00:33:10.546 --> 00:33:13.770
you need to look at
many diffusion dimensions to

00:33:13.771 --> 00:33:18.035
get at this even though it
has some of this information.

00:33:18.036 --> 00:33:20.710
This is how the PHATE
algorithm works.

00:33:20.711 --> 00:33:22.195
So, the first three
steps are similar

00:33:22.196 --> 00:33:24.014
to the steps that you saw before,

00:33:24.015 --> 00:33:25.310
which is, you have the data,

00:33:25.311 --> 00:33:27.485
you learn distances
and affinities,

00:33:27.486 --> 00:33:29.590
and you power the matrix,

00:33:29.591 --> 00:33:31.620
although usually to
a higher power because

00:33:31.621 --> 00:33:34.335
you're visualizing in
two or three dimensions.

00:33:34.336 --> 00:33:35.660
But, then, we use

00:33:35.661 --> 00:33:38.220
some information theoretic
techniques after that.

00:33:38.221 --> 00:33:40.470
So, after that, we
realized that we want to

00:33:40.471 --> 00:33:43.440
really preserve the
information in this matrix,

00:33:43.441 --> 00:33:45.020
information theoretically as much

00:33:45.021 --> 00:33:46.880
as possible in low dimensions.

00:33:46.881 --> 00:33:49.460
So, we come up with an
information theoretic distance

00:33:49.461 --> 00:33:52.535
between these diffusion
probabilities.

00:33:52.536 --> 00:33:56.545
Then, again, we don't want
to lose any information,

00:33:56.546 --> 00:33:58.425
so instead of just visualizing

00:33:58.426 --> 00:34:00.370
eigenvector one versus
eigenvector two,

00:34:00.371 --> 00:34:01.800
which is what people do in PCA,

00:34:01.801 --> 00:34:03.630
we squeeze all the variability in

00:34:03.631 --> 00:34:05.685
add it to dimensions by running

00:34:05.686 --> 00:34:07.630
metric MDS on

00:34:07.631 --> 00:34:10.180
these informational distances
that we came up with.

00:34:10.181 --> 00:34:11.840
So, we're making sure

00:34:11.841 --> 00:34:13.700
that we don't leave
any information out

00:34:13.701 --> 00:34:15.730
there that we haven't
captured and that we're

00:34:15.731 --> 00:34:18.765
capturing as much as
possible in our distances.

00:34:18.766 --> 00:34:23.060
The idea of using an
information theoretic distance

00:34:23.061 --> 00:34:26.760
is that each point is
a probability distribution,

00:34:26.761 --> 00:34:28.540
each point is
a distribution between

00:34:28.541 --> 00:34:32.665
affinities or
probabilities of walking.

00:34:32.666 --> 00:34:34.790
If you just took this
straight pointwise distance,

00:34:34.791 --> 00:34:37.080
it would be dominated by
what's happening with

00:34:37.081 --> 00:34:38.410
the nearest neighbors
and it would

00:34:38.411 --> 00:34:40.495
look a lot more like tSNE.

00:34:40.496 --> 00:34:43.470
But if you use an information
theoretic distance,

00:34:43.471 --> 00:34:44.990
usually information
theoretic distances

00:34:44.991 --> 00:34:45.970
have a damping factor.

00:34:45.971 --> 00:34:49.960
So, you have a log as you
do in Shannon entropy.

00:34:49.961 --> 00:34:51.170
So, there's damping factor makes

00:34:51.171 --> 00:34:52.960
the low-probability differences

00:34:52.961 --> 00:34:57.135
for changes also picked
up by the distance.

00:34:57.136 --> 00:34:59.440
The idea, again, of using

00:34:59.441 --> 00:35:01.790
metric MDS instead of
Eigendecomposition

00:35:01.791 --> 00:35:03.470
is that we only get

00:35:03.471 --> 00:35:06.690
two or three or however
many dimensions you want.

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:09.880
It's doing the best job
it can to preserve

00:35:09.881 --> 00:35:11.930
all this information
in low dimensions.

00:35:11.931 --> 00:35:13.760
So, that gives you PHATE.

00:35:13.761 --> 00:35:17.290
Here, again, these are
ground-truth examples,

00:35:17.291 --> 00:35:20.085
where we generated the stuff
that's on the first column.

00:35:20.086 --> 00:35:23.270
We generated this and
we see what PHATE is

00:35:23.271 --> 00:35:26.625
giving us versus what
other things are giving us.

00:35:26.626 --> 00:35:29.025
So, sometimes the
other methods do pretty well

00:35:29.026 --> 00:35:32.330
on turitive like
the Gaussian mixture model.

00:35:32.331 --> 00:35:34.135
Sometimes they again, do weirdly,

00:35:34.136 --> 00:35:36.625
like tSNE is putting the
green and red in between

00:35:36.626 --> 00:35:40.165
the blue and orangeish color.

00:35:40.166 --> 00:35:41.710
Sometimes they are very noisy,

00:35:41.711 --> 00:35:44.390
like isomap can be
very noisy because

00:35:44.391 --> 00:35:48.020
it's doing geodesic distances,
shortest path distances.

00:35:48.021 --> 00:35:49.470
Shortest path distance will take

00:35:49.471 --> 00:35:52.730
every shortcut they can throw
every outlier they can.

00:35:52.890 --> 00:35:55.590
You can see things like
force-directed layout that

00:35:55.591 --> 00:35:57.530
are just trying to
split things out.

00:35:57.531 --> 00:35:59.305
Then you get what?

00:35:59.306 --> 00:36:01.555
One of my students called
sneeze spots from it,

00:36:01.556 --> 00:36:03.955
it looks like a little point
is sneezed out.

00:36:03.956 --> 00:36:05.420
There's other methods
called Monocle

00:36:05.421 --> 00:36:06.950
that fit trees to data.

00:36:06.951 --> 00:36:09.630
It's not quite appropriate
but some reviewers asked us

00:36:09.631 --> 00:36:13.140
to compare against that.

00:36:13.141 --> 00:36:15.110
Then, you can
imagine that it does

00:36:15.111 --> 00:36:17.020
the same thing to
biological data.

00:36:17.021 --> 00:36:20.025
So, there's different types
of data we visualize here.

00:36:20.026 --> 00:36:21.700
The first is our embryonic

00:36:21.701 --> 00:36:23.225
stem cell differentiation data.

00:36:23.226 --> 00:36:26.870
The second is
retinal bipolar data,

00:36:26.871 --> 00:36:30.390
which they mainly thought
had a lot of clusters

00:36:30.391 --> 00:36:31.930
but we can see that the clusters

00:36:31.931 --> 00:36:34.475
have some progression
within them.

00:36:34.476 --> 00:36:36.450
Then, there's

00:36:36.451 --> 00:36:40.140
hematopoietic stem cell
differentiation data in A.

00:36:40.141 --> 00:36:43.890
There's IPSE reprogramming in
E. When they're grayed out,

00:36:43.891 --> 00:36:45.460
it means those methods
don't scale,

00:36:45.461 --> 00:36:48.435
so we had to sample
them to begin with,

00:36:48.436 --> 00:36:51.190
which turns out to be a huge
problem for most of these,

00:36:51.191 --> 00:36:53.300
where there is kind of
diffusion process can be

00:36:53.301 --> 00:36:56.520
made really fast
using some tricks.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:37:01.330
So, this is a visualization

00:37:01.331 --> 00:37:03.010
of what PHATE was trying to do.

00:37:03.011 --> 00:37:06.450
It's trying to keep
global structure like PCA.

00:37:06.451 --> 00:37:08.190
There is also clarifying
local structure

00:37:08.191 --> 00:37:09.220
because they can denoise

00:37:09.221 --> 00:37:11.100
a non-linear
interactions and find

00:37:11.101 --> 00:37:13.830
non-linear frictions
of dimensionalities,

00:37:13.831 --> 00:37:17.015
so you get more fine grained
detail than you would.

00:37:17.016 --> 00:37:18.550
You can zoom in on

00:37:18.551 --> 00:37:21.360
those fine details and
I have a zoom-in slide.

00:37:21.361 --> 00:37:23.530
But, we can show, for example,

00:37:23.531 --> 00:37:25.265
that this orange branch that

00:37:25.266 --> 00:37:27.300
the way it's splitting
is actually real and

00:37:27.301 --> 00:37:30.080
there's some biological
conclusions about what

00:37:30.081 --> 00:37:33.755
the differences between those
two retinal cell types are.

00:37:33.756 --> 00:37:36.360
So, what can we do
after we run a PHATE?

00:37:36.361 --> 00:37:37.670
You can do a lot of
different things.

00:37:37.671 --> 00:37:39.660
You can cluster around
the PHATE dimensions.

00:37:39.661 --> 00:37:41.980
We compute intrinsic
dimensionality

00:37:41.981 --> 00:37:44.015
to find branching points.

00:37:44.016 --> 00:37:47.490
If you have a lot of 1D
trajectories in your data,

00:37:47.491 --> 00:37:51.045
branching points have
higher dimension locally,

00:37:51.046 --> 00:37:54.680
we can use these branching
points in conjunction with

00:37:54.681 --> 00:37:55.845
the clusters to come up with

00:37:55.846 --> 00:37:58.545
lineages or regions of analysis.

00:37:58.546 --> 00:38:00.900
So, we gave this analysis

00:38:00.901 --> 00:38:04.180
to our collaborator at
the Yale Stem Cell Center.

00:38:04.181 --> 00:38:06.640
She was able to
play with this tool

00:38:06.641 --> 00:38:08.560
and identify a lot of
different lineages.

00:38:08.561 --> 00:38:11.585
So, she came up with
this tree of lineages.

00:38:11.586 --> 00:38:13.510
There are several intermediates

00:38:13.511 --> 00:38:14.800
here that she did not know.

00:38:14.801 --> 00:38:16.865
So, this is truly a tool for

00:38:16.866 --> 00:38:19.450
people to visualize
their own data from

00:38:19.451 --> 00:38:22.190
their own domain and
figure out what's

00:38:22.191 --> 00:38:25.440
in the data and use it
for further analysis.

00:38:25.441 --> 00:38:27.300
So, we were able to sort out

00:38:27.301 --> 00:38:29.320
some of the populations
that she was interested

00:38:29.321 --> 00:38:30.810
in and then you can
in fact sort them

00:38:30.811 --> 00:38:34.400
and figure out what
to do with it next.

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:38.380
So, the final project
that I'll talk about,

00:38:38.381 --> 00:38:40.365
it ties all of
these things together.

00:38:40.366 --> 00:38:41.740
&gt;&gt; Can I ask
one question about this?

00:38:41.741 --> 00:38:42.205
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:38:42.206 --> 00:38:44.765
&gt;&gt; So, it seems like
there are two big things.

00:38:44.766 --> 00:38:48.865
One is using the divisions
to do this moving,

00:38:48.866 --> 00:38:50.975
another one is, you replace.

00:38:50.976 --> 00:38:52.885
&gt;&gt; To do, yes, go ahead.

00:38:52.886 --> 00:38:55.745
&gt;&gt; Then, the other one is
you use this information of

00:38:55.746 --> 00:38:57.380
divergence towards the data than

00:38:57.381 --> 00:38:59.750
you believe in. I
was just saying.

00:38:59.751 --> 00:39:00.650
&gt;&gt; It's okay.

00:39:00.651 --> 00:39:02.000
&gt;&gt; So, I'm wondering whether,

00:39:02.001 --> 00:39:04.040
have you guys tried to apply

00:39:04.041 --> 00:39:06.495
the two aspect and see which one?

00:39:06.496 --> 00:39:09.050
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, actually, all
these steps are essential,

00:39:09.051 --> 00:39:10.430
there isn't random collection.

00:39:10.431 --> 00:39:12.260
Each of these steps is essential

00:39:12.261 --> 00:39:14.100
for getting the end results.

00:39:14.101 --> 00:39:16.230
So, if you remove
the fusion, it doesn't work.

00:39:16.231 --> 00:39:18.320
If you remove the information,
this thing doesn't work.

00:39:18.321 --> 00:39:20.840
So, all of these are
essential steps.

00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:24.400
&gt;&gt; We've validated
that every step

00:39:24.401 --> 00:39:25.825
is basically necessary.

00:39:25.826 --> 00:39:28.255
Would we still have
that figure in America paper?

00:39:28.256 --> 00:39:30.490
Now, there's always these
constraints when you submit.

00:39:30.491 --> 00:39:33.310
&gt;&gt; I'm just wondering
because people use this need

00:39:33.311 --> 00:39:36.540
for gene expression
clustering, and there,

00:39:36.541 --> 00:39:39.115
I can see the one big
difference is that

00:39:39.116 --> 00:39:43.320
the chest group count

00:39:43.321 --> 00:39:47.395
is noise to noise as
this thing, right?

00:39:47.396 --> 00:39:49.750
&gt;&gt; No, it is. It can be.

00:39:49.751 --> 00:39:53.740
So, I think that
these needs may be

00:39:53.741 --> 00:39:55.734
okay for some kind of clustering

00:39:55.735 --> 00:39:58.780
because it keeps
local neighbors together,

00:39:58.781 --> 00:40:00.715
but if your data doesn't

00:40:00.716 --> 00:40:02.530
have that kind of
clustering structure

00:40:02.531 --> 00:40:04.925
and it keeps them

00:40:04.926 --> 00:40:06.920
together at a very
particular granularity.

00:40:06.921 --> 00:40:08.730
The granularity is determined

00:40:08.731 --> 00:40:10.885
by the perplexity
you set in Teasne.

00:40:10.886 --> 00:40:13.510
So, basically, at
that level of granularity,

00:40:13.511 --> 00:40:15.480
it's splitting your data
onto clusters,

00:40:15.481 --> 00:40:17.715
and I think that's
an okay use-case for Teasne.

00:40:17.716 --> 00:40:20.280
But this is not an okay in
this case if you're trying to

00:40:20.281 --> 00:40:23.140
find the relationships
between your populations,

00:40:23.141 --> 00:40:25.870
which clusters are actually
close to each other,

00:40:25.871 --> 00:40:30.260
because also the KL divergence
penalty is very lopsided.

00:40:30.261 --> 00:40:33.250
It cares if you put
close neighbors far,

00:40:33.251 --> 00:40:35.630
but there's no effective
penalty for putting

00:40:35.631 --> 00:40:38.770
far things closer in
any configuration.

00:40:38.771 --> 00:40:42.030
But what Teasne does that
PCA doesn't do, for example,

00:40:42.031 --> 00:40:44.330
is it does squeeze variability
into two dimensions,

00:40:44.331 --> 00:40:46.950
but it does it with
stochastic gradient descent.

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:49.720
So, that's a little random.

00:40:49.721 --> 00:40:51.830
So, if you run Teasne many times,

00:40:51.831 --> 00:40:54.120
you'll get different results.

00:40:57.660 --> 00:41:02.050
So, the next project
I'll talk about is,

00:41:02.051 --> 00:41:03.760
so I've talked a lot
about how really

00:41:03.761 --> 00:41:05.130
understanding the
shape of the manifold

00:41:05.131 --> 00:41:06.349
helps you visualize,

00:41:06.350 --> 00:41:07.780
helps you figure
out the differences

00:41:07.781 --> 00:41:09.070
between experimental conditions,

00:41:09.071 --> 00:41:11.215
it helps you restore transcripts.

00:41:11.216 --> 00:41:14.410
So, can we do some of
this with Deep Learning,

00:41:14.411 --> 00:41:16.200
which adds to it
all the other features

00:41:16.201 --> 00:41:17.655
that deep learning has,

00:41:17.656 --> 00:41:18.960
which has even increased

00:41:18.961 --> 00:41:21.570
scalability and the ability
to do many things at

00:41:21.571 --> 00:41:22.700
the same time because you have

00:41:22.701 --> 00:41:26.670
some deep model through which
you can process your data.

00:41:26.671 --> 00:41:28.140
&gt;&gt; By the way,

00:41:28.141 --> 00:41:30.370
Fade is very scalable.

00:41:30.371 --> 00:41:32.825
&gt;&gt; Fade and Magic are
both very scalable,

00:41:32.826 --> 00:41:35.844
and David worked a lot on
making them superscalable,

00:41:35.845 --> 00:41:39.070
so I'm sure he wants
me to mention that.

00:41:39.071 --> 00:41:42.390
But I'm sure you guys know
that in deep learning,

00:41:42.391 --> 00:41:45.080
you can form it out to
a bunch of GPUs or something.

00:41:45.081 --> 00:41:48.745
The more GPUs you have
to parallelize this,

00:41:48.746 --> 00:41:51.760
but if you have a lot
of CPUs, it's fine.

00:41:51.761 --> 00:41:56.170
So, SAUCIE is an auto-encoder
based framework,

00:41:56.171 --> 00:41:58.505
and that's what the A stands for,

00:41:58.506 --> 00:42:01.990
so we think really hard
about making these acronyms.

00:42:01.991 --> 00:42:06.850
So, the idea of

00:42:06.851 --> 00:42:08.930
SAUCIE is actually to
find these kind of

00:42:08.931 --> 00:42:11.520
patterns emergently
in a neural network,

00:42:11.521 --> 00:42:14.750
instead of using
some kind of diffusion

00:42:14.751 --> 00:42:16.255
based algorithms based on

00:42:16.256 --> 00:42:19.060
constraints and regularizations.

00:42:20.290 --> 00:42:24.130
What we're trying to
do with SAUCIE is,

00:42:24.131 --> 00:42:26.350
we're trying to go to the realm

00:42:26.351 --> 00:42:29.110
where there's
massive amounts of data.

00:42:29.111 --> 00:42:31.120
Jonathan was saying
today that there's

00:42:31.121 --> 00:42:33.145
massive amounts of
TCR data, for example.

00:42:33.146 --> 00:42:34.985
So, if we have
massive amounts of data,

00:42:34.986 --> 00:42:36.540
like single-cell data
collected on many,

00:42:36.541 --> 00:42:38.350
many samples, we want to be able

00:42:38.351 --> 00:42:40.320
to run it through
the system, and come out,

00:42:40.321 --> 00:42:43.605
and read a lot of
patterns of the fact that

00:42:43.606 --> 00:42:46.290
an Auto-encoder
Neural Network is essentially

00:42:46.291 --> 00:42:48.200
a non-linear dimensionality
reduction as

00:42:48.201 --> 00:42:50.170
long as you have
non-linear activations.

00:42:50.171 --> 00:42:58.345
So, some of the things that
we designed SAUCIE to do are,

00:42:58.346 --> 00:43:01.470
from the ways that different
layers are regularized,

00:43:01.471 --> 00:43:03.135
we can read these patterns
off the data.

00:43:03.136 --> 00:43:05.250
So, actually, people
always complain

00:43:05.251 --> 00:43:06.320
that you don't know
what the inside of

00:43:06.321 --> 00:43:07.480
a neural network is doing,

00:43:07.481 --> 00:43:08.945
and that's not true
in our case of

00:43:08.946 --> 00:43:11.475
our neural network
because the outside,

00:43:11.476 --> 00:43:12.930
it's doing one interesting thing,

00:43:12.931 --> 00:43:14.100
which is de-noising data,

00:43:14.101 --> 00:43:16.450
but other than that, it's just
recreating it's own input.

00:43:16.451 --> 00:43:17.930
That's what an auto-encoder does.

00:43:17.931 --> 00:43:21.370
The internal layers are
good for visualization.

00:43:21.371 --> 00:43:23.950
We can read clusters
off one of the layers.

00:43:23.951 --> 00:43:26.765
We can batch align or
batch normalize them.

00:43:26.766 --> 00:43:29.110
Then, we can also
recreate the output.

00:43:29.111 --> 00:43:30.355
So, those are
the different layers.

00:43:30.356 --> 00:43:32.620
Then finally, it gives

00:43:32.621 --> 00:43:34.960
you the non-linear
dimensionality reduction.

00:43:34.961 --> 00:43:37.020
On particular patients,
it can give you

00:43:37.021 --> 00:43:41.025
stratifications of
the patients themselves.

00:43:41.026 --> 00:43:43.430
So, I guess you

00:43:43.431 --> 00:43:45.490
don't need an explanation
of deep neural networks.

00:43:45.491 --> 00:43:48.500
An auto-encoder was first
introduced many years ago.

00:43:48.501 --> 00:43:50.015
I don't know when it
was first introduced.

00:43:50.016 --> 00:43:54.125
It was popularized in
the science paper in 2006,

00:43:54.126 --> 00:43:57.180
and the idea is very simple
that the only penalty that

00:43:57.181 --> 00:44:00.250
the neural network
natively enforces

00:44:00.251 --> 00:44:03.805
is that the input be
recreated as the output,

00:44:03.806 --> 00:44:05.860
and so this makes
it unsupervised.

00:44:05.861 --> 00:44:07.305
You don't need any labels.

00:44:07.306 --> 00:44:08.530
It means that if you

00:44:08.531 --> 00:44:11.320
have reduced dimensions
in the middle of that,

00:44:11.321 --> 00:44:14.915
they have some kind of
dimensionality reduction going on.

00:44:14.916 --> 00:44:19.860
So, this is more specifically
what SAUCIE looks like.

00:44:19.861 --> 00:44:24.550
So, SAUCIE has an MMD penalty

00:44:24.551 --> 00:44:27.605
or a penalty to align batches,

00:44:27.606 --> 00:44:30.770
enforced on it's
lowest dimensional layer.

00:44:30.771 --> 00:44:33.440
The idea here is that you

00:44:33.441 --> 00:44:34.480
treat the batches as

00:44:34.481 --> 00:44:36.540
different distributions
and align them.

00:44:36.541 --> 00:44:37.870
There's a layer that's

00:44:37.871 --> 00:44:40.585
two-dimensional that you
can use for visualization.

00:44:40.586 --> 00:44:43.610
There's a layer that has
a special regularization

00:44:43.611 --> 00:44:46.110
that we call the Information
Dimension Regularization

00:44:46.111 --> 00:44:49.255
that binarizes activations or

00:44:49.256 --> 00:44:51.900
makes them easily binarizable.

00:44:51.901 --> 00:44:54.545
There, you can read
clusters off the data.

00:44:54.546 --> 00:44:56.320
Finally, the output layer

00:44:56.321 --> 00:44:58.330
de-noises the data because
it's trying to recreate

00:44:58.331 --> 00:44:59.395
