methods like PCA,

00:46:23.271 --> 00:46:26.320
tSNE and things like that.

00:46:26.321 --> 00:46:29.275
You just take the two-dimensional
layer and visualize it.

00:46:29.276 --> 00:46:30.700
This the representation for

00:46:30.701 --> 00:46:33.020
the clustering to
the idea was this,

00:46:33.021 --> 00:46:35.830
if you have some
cluster of cells,

00:46:35.831 --> 00:46:39.615
you want to be able to read
off which cluster they're in.

00:46:39.616 --> 00:46:41.410
Then, within cluster variation,

00:46:41.411 --> 00:46:43.420
is only stored in

00:46:43.421 --> 00:46:46.205
a small range on top
of this binarization.

00:46:46.206 --> 00:46:48.155
It's still there because
it recreates it,

00:46:48.156 --> 00:46:51.090
but the majority
of the signal that

00:46:51.091 --> 00:46:54.460
these nodes are giving is
which cluster it's in.

00:46:54.461 --> 00:46:58.165
So, this particular cluster
is encoded by basically 011,

00:46:58.166 --> 00:47:00.060
and a different cluster

00:47:00.061 --> 00:47:02.080
could be encoded by
something different.

00:47:02.081 --> 00:47:04.780
So, the way you do this is,

00:47:04.781 --> 00:47:08.450
we penalize for
activation entropy

00:47:08.451 --> 00:47:10.615
as von Neumann Entropy.

00:47:10.616 --> 00:47:14.480
If you take all of your
activation values,

00:47:14.481 --> 00:47:17.720
and you treat them as
a probability distribution,

00:47:17.721 --> 00:47:20.455
and you compute
Shannon Entropy on it,

00:47:20.456 --> 00:47:22.440
if all your nodes are

00:47:22.441 --> 00:47:24.935
activated to
roughly the same level,

00:47:24.936 --> 00:47:27.025
then you'll have high

00:47:27.026 --> 00:47:29.200
von Neumann Entropy if
you can call it that.

00:47:29.201 --> 00:47:30.910
If one of them is one,

00:47:30.911 --> 00:47:32.895
and a lot of them are zeros

00:47:32.896 --> 00:47:35.480
or a few of them are ones
and a lot of them are zeros,

00:47:35.481 --> 00:47:37.795
and you have low
activation entropy.

00:47:37.796 --> 00:47:44.020
So, this gives you binarizable
entries or activation.

00:47:44.021 --> 00:47:45.590
So, with no regularization you

00:47:45.591 --> 00:47:47.790
see a lot of things are
activated middle, high.

00:47:47.791 --> 00:47:50.665
L1 Regularization, which
a lot of people use

00:47:50.666 --> 00:47:53.655
just avoids the very high value,

00:47:53.656 --> 00:47:55.120
and then they're pushed over

00:47:55.121 --> 00:47:57.195
and you still have
a lot of entropy there.

00:47:57.196 --> 00:48:00.645
But our regularization gives
you a binarizable shape.

00:48:00.646 --> 00:48:03.690
If you round it or
actually binarize it,

00:48:03.691 --> 00:48:05.830
you get cluster
level information rather than

00:48:05.831 --> 00:48:09.780
cell level information from this.

00:48:10.010 --> 00:48:12.300
That's the main idea of

00:48:12.301 --> 00:48:13.690
the regularization on the layer

00:48:13.691 --> 00:48:15.250
that reads off the clusters.

00:48:15.251 --> 00:48:16.965
For the batch effects,

00:48:16.966 --> 00:48:19.660
this is an embedding of patient
one versus patient two,

00:48:19.661 --> 00:48:21.855
and this is a Dengue
patient data set.

00:48:21.856 --> 00:48:26.305
We use a probabilistic distance.

00:48:26.306 --> 00:48:28.480
This been use a little bit
in Neural Network.

00:48:28.481 --> 00:48:29.230
It's been there too much just

00:48:29.231 --> 00:48:31.635
called Maximal Mean Discrepancy,

00:48:31.636 --> 00:48:34.630
and you can compute this
at the batch level.

00:48:34.631 --> 00:48:37.450
You're computing the batch one B

00:48:37.451 --> 00:48:42.295
have minimal probabilistic
distance to batch two.

00:48:42.296 --> 00:48:44.500
But it still has to reconstruct

00:48:44.501 --> 00:48:46.660
and do these other things.

00:48:46.661 --> 00:48:49.010
So, it encourages to

00:48:49.011 --> 00:48:52.305
minimally align the data and
not totally re-scramble it.

00:48:52.306 --> 00:48:56.145
So, for the red and the blue
are different patient

00:48:56.146 --> 00:49:00.345
so the embedding is mainly
organized by patient,

00:49:00.346 --> 00:49:02.235
which we don't want.

00:49:02.236 --> 00:49:04.160
We want the batch effects

00:49:04.161 --> 00:49:06.350
between when the patients
were measured,

00:49:06.351 --> 00:49:07.985
what time of the day
all of that to go away.

00:49:07.986 --> 00:49:11.300
So, after MMD, you see that
it's organized by cell type.

00:49:11.301 --> 00:49:14.050
So now, we can see how
my CD3 positive cells both

00:49:14.051 --> 00:49:18.035
these patients have and things
like that. I don't know.

00:49:18.036 --> 00:49:20.980
We tried this on
this Dengue data,

00:49:20.981 --> 00:49:23.180
it was from NIMHANS
in Bangalore is

00:49:23.181 --> 00:49:26.070
a Neuroradiology
Department in NIMHANS.

00:49:26.071 --> 00:49:27.395
So, they gave us.

00:49:27.396 --> 00:49:29.110
It's something like 60 patients,

00:49:29.111 --> 00:49:32.650
and they visited two times,

00:49:32.651 --> 00:49:35.800
and then there's
an additional number

00:49:35.801 --> 00:49:37.470
of patients who came with them,

00:49:37.471 --> 00:49:38.920
and they are
the healthy controls,

00:49:38.921 --> 00:49:40.680
were living in
the same environment.

00:49:40.681 --> 00:49:42.770
So, there were 180 files,

00:49:42.771 --> 00:49:44.220
each of them have many,

00:49:44.221 --> 00:49:45.800
many cells, and so,

00:49:45.801 --> 00:49:48.290
totally this data was
20 million cells.

00:49:48.291 --> 00:49:51.950
You had to batch normalized
between 180 different files,

00:49:51.951 --> 00:49:55.260
and there's the few
batch normalization tools

00:49:55.261 --> 00:49:58.160
that are out there don't really
normalize at this scale.

00:49:58.161 --> 00:50:01.640
They do two conditions
or something like that.

00:50:01.641 --> 00:50:04.180
You see what the cells look

00:50:04.181 --> 00:50:06.644
like if you combine
all the acutes,

00:50:06.645 --> 00:50:09.035
and there are healthy
versus the convalescent.

00:50:09.036 --> 00:50:10.470
Convalescent are acute people

00:50:10.471 --> 00:50:11.870
they came back some weeks later,

00:50:11.871 --> 00:50:13.860
and their cells are
starting to look a lot

00:50:13.861 --> 00:50:15.860
more like the healthy
just to inject.

00:50:15.861 --> 00:50:18.080
But you see that there's
no batch effect.

00:50:18.081 --> 00:50:19.740
You can see each of
the acute patients

00:50:19.741 --> 00:50:21.380
separately or anything like that,

00:50:21.381 --> 00:50:23.300
it's a unified manifold.

00:50:23.301 --> 00:50:24.860
On this unified manifold,

00:50:24.861 --> 00:50:27.395
you can go ahead and cluster
like it's one data set,

00:50:27.396 --> 00:50:29.750
which wasn't possible before.

00:50:29.751 --> 00:50:30.925
There's other methods that

00:50:30.926 --> 00:50:32.980
will cluster totally separately,

00:50:32.981 --> 00:50:34.480
and then try to
match these clusters

00:50:34.481 --> 00:50:36.495
and turns into
a huge huge problem.

00:50:36.496 --> 00:50:38.160
Instead, you can just cluster

00:50:38.161 --> 00:50:41.145
this data as if
it's one data set.

00:50:41.146 --> 00:50:45.770
You come up with
these populations of cells.

00:50:45.771 --> 00:50:49.460
We find that some of
the populations of cells,

00:50:49.461 --> 00:50:51.610
for example, are
very interesting.

00:50:51.611 --> 00:50:54.270
There's the TC or
Gamma Delta population,

00:50:54.271 --> 00:50:55.890
and we saw that a lot in

00:50:55.891 --> 00:50:58.170
the acutely infected
patients that they

00:50:58.171 --> 00:51:01.290
have NK cells on those.

00:51:01.291 --> 00:51:02.655
They have so many activity.

00:51:02.656 --> 00:51:04.510
They show signs of
innate activity,

00:51:04.511 --> 00:51:05.940
and this is a rare population,

00:51:05.941 --> 00:51:08.260
but we can still pick up
a systematic difference.

00:51:08.261 --> 00:51:12.140
Then, you can see
these signatures

00:51:12.141 --> 00:51:14.140
of these different cell types

00:51:14.141 --> 00:51:16.120
for the different patients
that are acute,

00:51:16.121 --> 00:51:20.360
convalescent or healthy,
and then you could embed it

00:51:20.361 --> 00:51:25.030
again with an MMD-based,
DMD-based distance.

00:51:25.031 --> 00:51:29.620
We see that all the patients
themselves are

00:51:29.621 --> 00:51:31.180
stratified where the left side

00:51:31.181 --> 00:51:32.280
of the embedding is a lot of

00:51:32.281 --> 00:51:35.190
the acute patients and
the right side is convalescent,

00:51:35.191 --> 00:51:36.600
and you see people
are convalescing to

00:51:36.601 --> 00:51:39.325
different extents and
things like that.

00:51:39.326 --> 00:51:41.570
Then you can associate this
with the proportions of

00:51:41.571 --> 00:51:45.950
different clusters that you
see in your clustering.

00:51:45.951 --> 00:51:48.200
Of course, the point that I was

00:51:48.201 --> 00:51:49.730
making is that you're
running all of

00:51:49.731 --> 00:51:51.960
this through
these neural networks

00:51:51.961 --> 00:51:54.250
that are tricked out or whatever,

00:51:54.251 --> 00:51:56.540
so it should be scalable,

00:51:56.541 --> 00:51:58.980
and we actually do find
that it's very scalable.

00:51:58.981 --> 00:52:01.250
We compare it against K-means

00:52:01.251 --> 00:52:03.645
and Phenograph
clustering methods.

00:52:03.646 --> 00:52:05.110
tSNE is a clustering,

00:52:05.111 --> 00:52:06.850
but also visualization method.

00:52:06.851 --> 00:52:08.980
PHATE, this is
the old PHATE sorry,

00:52:08.981 --> 00:52:12.070
compared against
the nonsupervised PHATE.

00:52:12.071 --> 00:52:15.505
PCA Neural Network, Completion,

00:52:15.506 --> 00:52:18.480
MNN is a type of
batch normalization,

00:52:18.481 --> 00:52:20.830
diffusion maps, all
of these things,

00:52:20.831 --> 00:52:23.190
and it's super, super fast.

00:52:23.191 --> 00:52:25.630
Faster than everything besides,

00:52:25.631 --> 00:52:28.860
I think it was PCA.

00:52:28.861 --> 00:52:30.930
That's because now
PCA is done with

00:52:30.931 --> 00:52:33.940
random projection matrix
is really quickly.

00:52:33.941 --> 00:52:36.570
The PCA doesn't give
you very good results.

00:52:36.571 --> 00:52:38.475
You randomly linearly projecting,

00:52:38.476 --> 00:52:40.070
you're losing a lot
of information.

00:52:40.071 --> 00:52:44.620
So, quality wise, SAUCIE is
way better than any kind of

00:52:44.621 --> 00:52:46.910
PCA because it does
so many other things and

00:52:46.911 --> 00:52:50.850
it's a non-linear method.

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:55.845
Finally, it's because I added

00:52:55.846 --> 00:52:57.250
one slide because Jonathan

00:52:57.251 --> 00:52:58.945
was talking a lot about dynamics.

00:52:58.946 --> 00:53:00.740
We have a neural
network that we're

00:53:00.741 --> 00:53:03.100
training also to predict
dynamics in these things,

00:53:03.101 --> 00:53:05.490
and this network is generative
and it's stochastic,

00:53:05.491 --> 00:53:08.640
so we're calling
this the transcoder

00:53:08.641 --> 00:53:11.290
and because PHATE and

00:53:11.291 --> 00:53:13.730
these other methods allow
us to pick up trajectories,

00:53:13.731 --> 00:53:14.860
we're hoping to mimic

00:53:14.861 --> 00:53:17.490
these trajectories and find
some deep representation

00:53:17.491 --> 00:53:21.570
of the logic that's used
to make these transitions.

00:53:21.571 --> 00:53:24.145
But that's an ongoing project.

00:53:24.146 --> 00:53:27.410
We're hoping that test
systems, evolution systems

00:53:27.411 --> 00:53:30.530
pertain folding systems
and things like that.

00:53:31.290 --> 00:53:34.405
Those are the main projects
that I want to talk about.

00:53:34.406 --> 00:53:36.600
Again, the main idea is we

00:53:36.601 --> 00:53:38.670
