00:56:39.261 --> 00:56:40.630
that this neural
network also learns

00:56:40.631 --> 00:56:42.610
the manifold without
diffusion and

00:56:42.611 --> 00:56:44.730
without us having to define
distance and other things.

00:56:44.731 --> 00:56:47.850
&gt;&gt; With some other types
of logout filters.

00:56:47.851 --> 00:56:52.090
It's just not incredibly
diffusion in the graph but-.

00:56:56.100 --> 00:56:58.320
&gt;&gt; We have a very similar thing

00:56:58.321 --> 00:57:00.440
where you'll have
a knowledge graph.

00:57:00.441 --> 00:57:01.620
Your knowledge graph could be

00:57:01.621 --> 00:57:04.085
a relation between the entity.

00:57:04.086 --> 00:57:06.290
Then traditional people use

00:57:06.291 --> 00:57:10.035
random walkthrough to compute
a diffusion probability.

00:57:10.036 --> 00:57:12.330
&gt;&gt; You mean like a word vectors
or something like that?

00:57:12.331 --> 00:57:16.560
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Nowadays,
we embed it into

00:57:16.561 --> 00:57:19.010
embedding and then you can

00:57:19.011 --> 00:57:20.460
compute any arbitrary thing

00:57:20.461 --> 00:57:22.155
without doing any random walk.

00:57:22.156 --> 00:57:23.850
Because a random walk
would be expensive.

00:57:23.851 --> 00:57:26.670
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:57:26.671 --> 00:57:28.965
Given that we have-
sorry about that.

00:57:28.966 --> 00:57:35.485
&gt;&gt; Yes. So you want to talk
about this slider here.

00:57:35.486 --> 00:57:37.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah, nice.

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:38.845
&gt;&gt; This one I think.

00:57:38.846 --> 00:57:42.955
&gt;&gt; So, instead of computing
all paths between points,

00:57:42.956 --> 00:57:45.550
we're constraining the paths to

00:57:45.551 --> 00:57:48.750
go through specific landmarks.

00:57:48.751 --> 00:57:50.580
We fix the number of landmarks.

00:57:50.581 --> 00:57:56.090
We pick enough of them to
accurately describe the graph.

00:57:56.660 --> 00:58:00.260
So now we essentially
get a linear place.

00:58:00.261 --> 00:58:02.045
&gt;&gt; So now, the
diffusion goes from

00:58:02.046 --> 00:58:04.830
original points to landmark
points to original points.

00:58:04.831 --> 00:58:09.220
But if you do the matrix
association correctly.

00:58:09.221 --> 00:58:11.880
&gt;&gt; The landmarks are chosen to be

00:58:11.881 --> 00:58:17.030
uniformly representing
the whole graph.

00:58:17.031 --> 00:58:20.315
So we use very
little information.

00:58:20.316 --> 00:58:21.780
But we're still going to exclude

00:58:21.781 --> 00:58:23.535
the vast majority of paths.

00:58:23.536 --> 00:58:26.410
&gt;&gt; So you literally
random sample them?

00:58:26.411 --> 00:58:27.170
&gt;&gt; No.

00:58:27.171 --> 00:58:30.765
&gt;&gt; We actually use
a spectral clustering approach.

00:58:30.766 --> 00:58:31.935
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:58:31.936 --> 00:58:35.430
&gt;&gt; Using fast random SDD.

00:58:35.431 --> 00:58:36.465
So it's all super-fast.

00:58:36.466 --> 00:58:37.065
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:58:37.066 --> 00:58:40.085
&gt;&gt; So essentially
the whole thing is linear.

00:58:40.086 --> 00:58:41.440
&gt;&gt; So it's almost
linear, a little

00:58:41.441 --> 00:58:43.215
bit linear random projection?

00:58:43.216 --> 00:58:47.079
&gt;&gt; Yeah. We're doing random
projection to big landmarks,

00:58:47.080 --> 00:58:51.830
and then we compute paths to
go through our landmarks.

00:58:51.831 --> 00:58:53.640
&gt;&gt; That's very intriguing.

00:58:54.810 --> 00:58:59.100
&gt;&gt; It works almost exactly with

00:58:59.101 --> 00:59:03.420
the same result as the
original search plane version.

00:59:03.421 --> 00:59:06.180
&gt;&gt; Okay. You can look
up PHATE done by by

00:59:06.181 --> 00:59:09.580
archiver Ron Sells Sneakpeek
and how he show

00:59:09.581 --> 00:59:11.680
these comparisons and thousands

00:59:11.681 --> 00:59:14.275
of supplemental figures
and something like that.

00:59:14.276 --> 00:59:17.720
&gt;&gt; Sp the speck thing
in the runtime there,

00:59:17.721 --> 00:59:19.280
is that when you're not

00:59:19.281 --> 00:59:21.315
using as many landmarks
as the points or?

00:59:21.316 --> 00:59:23.360
&gt;&gt; Exactly. That's
a very good observation.

00:59:23.361 --> 00:59:25.930
So, we use I

00:59:25.931 --> 00:59:28.365
think by default something
like 2,000 landmarks.

00:59:28.366 --> 00:59:31.095
So when you have fewer points
than that number,

00:59:31.096 --> 00:59:33.185
there's no point in doing this.

00:59:33.186 --> 00:59:39.370
Yeah. So only really large
numbers help to do this.

00:59:39.371 --> 00:59:41.710
That's exactly why
we have that photo.

00:59:41.711 --> 00:59:45.550
&gt;&gt; The other motivation for
SOS isn't just scalability.

00:59:45.551 --> 00:59:48.520
It's also that all these sets
are implemented on

00:59:48.521 --> 00:59:51.730
some unified representation
of the data.

00:59:51.731 --> 00:59:54.105
So we find that

00:59:54.106 --> 00:59:56.030
some clustering methods
they may represent

00:59:56.031 --> 00:59:58.550
the data's graph or
some kind of cloud,

00:59:58.551 --> 00:59:59.580
and then you have a batch

00:59:59.581 --> 01:00:01.020
normalization method
that represents

01:00:01.021 --> 01:00:04.080
data as canonical
correlation dimensions.

01:00:04.081 --> 01:00:06.500
There's something. There's
very disparate stuff.

01:00:06.501 --> 01:00:10.045
The different features
you're reading off the data

01:00:10.046 --> 01:00:12.100
are not made from
the same representation

01:00:12.101 --> 01:00:13.630
so they're not correlated
to each other.

01:00:13.631 --> 01:00:15.040
&gt;&gt; TC in your data,

01:00:15.041 --> 01:00:17.930
there's no guarantee that
TC picks up the same

01:00:17.931 --> 01:00:19.420
clustered as K-means because they

01:00:19.421 --> 01:00:21.875
have different algorithms
representations.

01:00:21.876 --> 01:00:23.650
That was one of the reasons
that we wanted to do this.

01:00:23.651 --> 01:00:25.270
&gt;&gt; We do stochastic
gradient this side.

01:00:25.271 --> 01:00:26.680
You pass the gradient
all the way back so

01:00:26.681 --> 01:00:28.150
all the layers are coordinating.

01:00:28.151 --> 01:00:32.150
&gt;&gt; Here we have
one representations that

01:00:32.151 --> 01:00:34.260
guarantees that you get

01:00:34.261 --> 01:00:36.300
the same clusters as
your visualization.

01:00:36.301 --> 01:00:40.140
&gt;&gt; So that clustering
where we use

01:00:40.141 --> 01:00:45.575
the information entropy to
measure the constraint,

01:00:45.576 --> 01:00:50.500
that's going to effectively
push you towards picking

01:00:50.501 --> 01:00:56.700
a single cluster at
that layer. Right?

01:00:56.701 --> 01:00:59.470
&gt;&gt; You're forcing the nodes

01:00:59.471 --> 01:01:01.705
to become binaries or digital.

01:01:01.706 --> 01:01:03.760
Either zeros or ones.

01:01:03.761 --> 01:01:07.870
&gt;&gt; Even moreso we force
them to be single one-

01:01:07.871 --> 01:01:09.080
&gt;&gt; Not a single one.

01:01:09.081 --> 01:01:11.025
&gt;&gt; A single one would
be like L1 sparsity.

01:01:11.026 --> 01:01:13.150
Sparsity it will just try

01:01:13.151 --> 01:01:15.115
to make as many nodes up to zero.

01:01:15.116 --> 01:01:16.585
We're not doing that. We want

01:01:16.586 --> 01:01:18.220
the nodes to be zeros or ones.

01:01:18.221 --> 01:01:20.110
We want them binary or digital.

01:01:20.111 --> 01:01:23.215
&gt;&gt; So they're being computed
on each node independently?

01:01:23.216 --> 01:01:25.540
&gt;&gt; Each node becomes

01:01:25.541 --> 01:01:28.900
a probability and then
we use Shannon entropy.

01:01:28.901 --> 01:01:30.640
We use Shannon entropy.

01:01:30.641 --> 01:01:32.770
&gt;&gt; I'm calling it a name
entropy because it's like

01:01:32.771 --> 01:01:35.080
an entropy of values
rather than probabilities.

01:01:35.081 --> 01:01:37.105
That's why I was calling it that.

01:01:37.106 --> 01:01:40.615
&gt;&gt; So within each node
or crossover node?

01:01:40.616 --> 01:01:42.220
&gt;&gt; Crossover.

01:01:42.221 --> 01:01:43.240
&gt;&gt; Per set.

01:01:43.241 --> 01:01:44.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's across the nodes.

01:01:44.591 --> 01:01:45.610
&gt;&gt; Across the nodes.

01:01:45.611 --> 01:01:48.145
&gt;&gt; Yeah. But what we don't
see that it gives us

01:01:48.146 --> 01:01:49.720
a single one because it's not

01:01:49.721 --> 01:01:51.665
specifically a sparsity penalty.

01:01:51.666 --> 01:01:57.235
So, usually the neural network
will not make it one hard.

01:01:57.236 --> 01:01:59.050
It'll make it low entropy

01:01:59.051 --> 01:02:00.590
given that it can
still reconstruct.

01:02:00.591 --> 01:02:04.260
So usually, the
vectors are 3-4-1s.

01:02:04.261 --> 01:02:06.730
&gt;&gt; Is it just one
you could put in

01:02:06.731 --> 01:02:10.555
the soft nodes where
you're pushing into one.

01:02:10.556 --> 01:02:14.185
&gt;&gt; Yeah. One-hot encoding.
You could do that.

01:02:14.186 --> 01:02:16.630
&gt;&gt; But we don't usually do that.

01:02:16.631 --> 01:02:18.340
We don't push it to one-hot.

01:02:18.341 --> 01:02:20.680
&gt;&gt; With one-hot you still have

01:02:20.681 --> 01:02:21.910
to pick the number of nodes

01:02:21.911 --> 01:02:23.605
which will give you
the number of clusters.

01:02:23.606 --> 01:02:28.720
In this case actually we don't-.

01:02:28.721 --> 01:02:29.950
&gt;&gt; We don't know the number of

01:02:29.951 --> 01:02:31.190
clusters that we are pairing.

01:02:31.191 --> 01:02:31.960
&gt;&gt; What I'm saying
here is we don't know

01:02:31.961 --> 01:02:32.830
the number of clusters,

01:02:32.831 --> 01:02:33.925
so we don't have to define.

01:02:33.926 --> 01:02:36.895
We've set the number of
clusters we get out of it.

01:02:36.896 --> 01:02:39.865
&gt;&gt; But we can overall tweak

01:02:39.866 --> 01:02:42.120
the granularity by upping

01:02:42.121 --> 01:02:43.750
this penalty or
downing this penalty.

01:02:43.751 --> 01:02:46.515
But we don't know
exactly how many like

01:02:46.516 --> 01:02:48.030
when we're saying
10K means give us

01:02:48.031 --> 01:02:50.025
10 clustering or something.

01:02:50.026 --> 01:02:53.930
&gt;&gt; So how do you set
this coefficient

01:02:53.931 --> 01:02:55.825
for the regularization right now?

01:02:55.826 --> 01:02:58.390
&gt;&gt; So that's a variable.

01:02:58.391 --> 01:03:00.240
It depends on if you
want really big clusters

01:03:00.241 --> 01:03:02.760
like T-cells versus
B-cells versus monocytes.

01:03:02.761 --> 01:03:03.920
Or if you want to go inside

01:03:03.921 --> 01:03:06.070
the T-cells and see
the different sub textures.

01:03:06.071 --> 01:03:08.660
&gt;&gt; Also that's pretty robust.
We see that we change

01:03:08.661 --> 01:03:10.050
its biologies

01:03:10.051 --> 01:03:13.120
pretty similar or whatever
test cases we have.

01:03:13.121 --> 01:03:15.025
Gaussian mixtures or whatever.

01:03:15.026 --> 01:03:18.410
So it's pretty robust
against those preferences.

